A student connects two identical lamps to a battery, first in series and then in parallel.
Describe how the current and the voltage behave in each circuit, and explain why the lights in a house are connected in parallel rather than in series.
Your answer should include:
• what an ammeter reads at different points of the series circuit, and what the voltages across the two lamps add up to;
• what a voltmeter reads across each lamp in the parallel circuit, and how the current in the main line is related to the branch currents;
• what happens to the other lamp in each circuit when one lamp is removed;
• why house lights are wired in parallel.
This question also assesses the quality of your written communication, including your use of specialist scientific terms.
